From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Meta: Solomonic Wisdom Needed

A pleasant surprise: I'm not going to waste anyone's time responding to
Sandy's post on a point-by-point basis.  I think Sandy either misunderstood
or misrepresented what I said about list authoritarianism, but I have more
interesting things to discuss.

As I noted earlier, anyone who wants to set up their own list code is free
to do so.  They need only notify Harry if and when they need to borrow his
enforcement powers.  I look forward eagerly to seeing some PPLs arise
within the list's PPL.

As far as I can tell, no one has yet used a private^2 judge, however. 
After Sandy's most recent post, I again wondered why no one has opted for a
list tzar.

Sandy was replying to my admittedly sarcastic hypothesis that most folks
want to give tzars jurisdiction only over other people.  There's some truth
to this, but other lists with tzars (like cyberpunks, apparently) do get
plenty of traffic.  So people do sometimes choose to subject themselves to
virtual authoritarians.  Why?  They probably trust that the tzar will
subject everyone to the same useful rules, and do not risk anything more
than access to a virtual forum.  Realspace authoritarians, on the other
hand, find it harder to attract customers.

Those who call for a list tzar here may thus object that they do not want
to subject themselves to constraints that won't apply equally to
everyone--especially to folks like Singh.  But I note that they are not
entirely without the power to effect their own, private sanctions against
people who annoy them but who refuse to submit to their tzar's rules.  

They can, for instance, agree to monitor offensive people and to make sure
that Harry gets complaints about offensive posts.  And they can form a pact
to ostracize people they disfavor.

What's my point?  I want to encourage those who favor list authoritarianism
to go ahead and try it out--amongst themselves.  I'm arguing that they do
not need to subject all of us to the jurisdiction of their favorite tzar in
order to accrue (at least some of) the benefits of a benevolent
dictatorship.

Regardless of how well a benevolent dictatorship would work within such
private^2 legal systems or on other lists, though, I don't think it would
suit this list's goals.  We want to encourage experimentation with PPLs. 
But "because I say so" doesn't make for very interesting material.  Better,
I think, that we should put up with a bit more friction in order to gain
the benefits of experimental data, a rule of law, and rights to appeal.  If
more people will avail themselves of the opportunity to contract around the
list's default legal system, things will run even smoother yet.

Summary: Stop griping and start PPLing!

