Good point.  Though this may not be true in fact, it should be.  That is, I
think we all agree that property owners should be free to boot folks as
they see fit.  In unfortunate practice, however, statists have claimed the
right to say who business owners may and may not arbitrarily expel.  

There is still a separate question as to whether, as I claimed, realspace
authoritarians find it "harder" (not "impossible") to attract customers
than virtual ones, but this question doesn't interest me at present.  The
point is that there is nothing per se wrong with vesting someone with
absolute authority over the disposition of certain property or services. 
Sandy's post notwithstanding, I never claimed otherwise.  I just don't we
would benefit from giving one person absolute power over this list.

>Still, I think little PPL experiments would be fun (I joined
>ALL of the little societies)... um, where are they?
>
>derek

I wonder if transactions costs are too high.  After all, people sign up for
the list's default rules simply by subscribing.  Setting up another PPL
takes extra steps.  

And the incentives may be skewed.  Flamers like to subject us to their
ranting.  Why should they take the trouble to contract around the list's
rules?  Hence, I suppose, an argument for a list tzar.  But I remain
unconvinced that the costs of authoritarianism are worth the benefits. 
Better we should foster a list culture that encourages people to set up and
join PPLs, and that pushes a precommitment strategy whereby everyone
benefits from speedy and informal adjudication.

I guess, then, that I should volunteer to act as a private^2 judge.  OK.  I
do: I agree to adjudicate list disputes among consenting parties.
 



